<p class="doc-muted"><strong>Standing constraint:</strong> <code>.20</code> is <strong>database-only</strong> by decision - do not co-locate a registry, node, or any other service on it. <strong>Corrected 2026-08-11:</strong> earlier text claimed .198 "could not be probed (different SSH credentials)". That was wrong - .198 uses the <em>same</em> SSH credentials as .199 and was successfully probed; its disk was 100%-full of <em>personal data, not the database</em> and is now cleared (47&nbsp;GB free). .198 is a credible third node and registry host.</p>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#why">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>

<h2 id="target" class="doc-h2">2. Target architecture - Kubernetes is the PRIMARY path</h2>
<p class="doc-body">The endpoint is <strong>standard Kubernetes</strong>. k3s (if used for the control plane) is certified, conformant Kubernetes in a small package; everything authored - Deployments, Services, Ingress, Secrets, CronJobs - is standard K8s YAML. <strong>Because an existing cluster is already registered (.50), we extend THAT rather than standing up a second one.</strong> This is the path that already partially exists; v1.x of this plan erroneously demoted it to "Phase H (conditional / likely never needed)". That contradiction is removed.</p>
<div class="doc-info">
    <p class="doc-body"><strong>OPNsense is NOT the orchestrator.</strong> The app's OPNsense API client (<code>lib/Comserv/Util/Opnsense.pm</code>, <code>config/infrastructure/opnsense.json</code>) exists, but its HAProxy failover is <strong>not configured or working today</strong>. OPNsense, if used at all, is only an <strong>edge / ingress</strong> option and ONLY once actually configured. It is never a substitute for cluster self-healing.</p>
</div>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#why">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>

<h2 id="roadmap" class="doc-h2">3. The Step roadmap (one sequence, one vocabulary)</h2>
<p class="doc-body">Steps are strictly ordered. <strong>Step 0 is mandatory and first</strong> - without detectability every later step is blind. Each Step states its <strong>goal</strong>, its <strong>what</strong>, and its <strong>"done when"</strong> gate. A Step's <em>why-next</em> is the specific limitation that justifies the following Step; if a Step's why-next does not actually hurt us, we stop.</p>

<h3 id="step0" class="doc-h3">Step 0 - Detectability (logging + alerting that the app is down)</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Goal:</strong> an admin is told the moment the app or a node is offline. <strong>Why first:</strong> today nothing monitors the single app container; the 2026-08-07 outage served an nginx default page for ~100 minutes with no signal. Every later Step is unverifiable without this.</p>
<ul class="doc-body">
    <li>Deploy a lightweight probe (e.g. blackbox/HTTP check or a cron-driven health poll) that hits the app's health endpoint from off-box and alerts on failure.</li>
    <li>Node-level liveness: alert if a Kubernetes node (or the .126 app container pre-cutover) stops responding.</li>
    <li>Route alerts to a channel an admin actually watches (the app already has an email/alert path - reuse it; do not invent a new one).</li>
</ul>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Done when:</strong> killing the app container (or pulling a node's network) produces a real admin alert within minutes, and that alert is recorded in the changelog as proven.</p>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#why">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>

<h3 id="step1" class="doc-h3">Step 1 - Cluster (extend the existing Kubernetes cluster)</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Goal:</strong> a 3-node (or more) Kubernetes cluster using the <strong>existing</strong> <code>comserv-k8s-cluster</code> (.50) as the control plane, with <strong>.126, .198, .199</strong> joined as worker nodes. <strong>Why:</strong> a registry alone still needs a human to run the image per host; a cluster self-places and self-heals.</p>
<ul class="doc-body">
    <li>Verify the .50 control plane is reachable and healthy using <code>/home/shanta/.kube/comserv-k8s-config</code>; if it is dead, recover or re-bootstrap it before adding nodes (do not stand up a competing cluster).</li>
    <li>Join .126, .198, .199 as agents. <strong>.126 needs a coordinated dockerd/containerd restart</strong> to accept the node role - this is the one step that touches the live app container and needs explicit go-ahead.</li>
    <li>Label nodes by capability; taint .20 (database-only) so no general workload lands there.</li>
    <li>Verify no port collision with the live app (:5000) or the registry (:5001).</li>
</ul>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Done when:</strong> <code>kubectl get nodes</code> shows .126/.198/.199 <strong>Ready</strong>, and the existing compose app container on .126 is still serving untouched.</p>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#why">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>

<h3 id="step2" class="doc-h3">Step 2 - Registry (primary .198:5001 + fallback .199:5001)</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Goal:</strong> one private registry every node pulls from, with a fallback so a single registry host is not a hard stop. <strong>Why:</strong> without a shared image source, no node can pull a build automatically.</p>
<ul class="doc-body">
    <li>Primary registry: <code>registry:2</code> on <strong>.198</strong>, host port <strong>5001</strong> (5000 is taken by the live app container), volume <code>comserv2_registry_data</code>.</li>
    <li>Fallback registry: same on <strong>.199</strong> host port 5001. Push every built image to <strong>both</strong> so either can serve a pull.</li>
    <li>Canonical name <code>comserv-registry.lan:5001</code> &rarr; .198 (and a second name for .199). Use the name - never the bare IP - in every image tag and in every node's <code>insecure-registries</code>. The value must match byte-for-byte (an IP and a hostname are not interchangeable to the daemon).</li>
    <li>Record the canonical URL in <code>config/services.json</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Done when:</strong> a test tag pushed to primary AND fallback pulls successfully on a worker, with matching digests recorded.</p>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#why">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>

<div class="doc-info" id="registry">
    <h3 class="doc-h3">3c. Registry fallback - the ".198 reboot" question, answered</h3>
    <p class="doc-body"><strong>Does .198 have to be always running?</strong> No. The registry is needed only at <strong>pull time, not run time</strong>. Once a node has pulled the image, its container keeps serving even if the registry is down. So if .198 reboots for updates, already-running containers on .126/.199 keep serving; only new pulls (new deploys, or a rescheduled pod that lost its cached image) are blocked.</p>
    <p class="doc-body"><strong>Can we have more than one?</strong> Yes - and we should. Two registries (.198:5001 primary, .199:5001 fallback) don't auto-sync, so we push each image to both. A node's <code>insecure-registries</code> lists both; if .198's is down, pull from .199's. The destructive prune in deploy.sh is already neutered, and images are cached per node, so even a total registry outage doesn't kill running nodes - only a cold start on a fresh node would need the registry.</p>
</div>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#why">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>

<h3 id="step3" class="doc-h3">Step 3 - Deployment (replicas&gt;=2, anti-affinity, tuned probes)</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Goal:</strong> the app as a Kubernetes Deployment, <strong>replicas&gt;=2 spread across nodes</strong> (anti-affinity), with probes tuned to the real <strong>~90s</strong> startup. <strong>Why:</strong> a scheduler with no declared desired state cannot self-heal or roll out.</p>
<ul class="doc-body">
    <li>Deployment (<code>replicas: 2</code>, image <code>&lt;registry&gt;/comserv-web-prod:&lt;sha&gt;</code>), Service (ClusterIP), Ingress with health probe on <code>/health</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Probes:</strong> liveness/readiness <code>start_period</code> 60s+, because the app genuinely needs ~90s to become healthy (measured 2026-08-07). Too-short probes recreate the exact kill-loop that caused the outage.</li>
    <li><strong>Secrets:</strong> DB credentials via a Kubernetes Secret sourced from <code>db_config.json</code> - never baked into the image.</li>
    <li><strong>Shared state must be external to the pod:</strong> NFS .175 (<code>workshop-files-nfs</code> &rarr; container <code>/data/nfs</code>); the mount travels with the Deployment spec and requires .175's <code>/etc/exports</code> to permit each node's IP. A bare <code>docker run</code> missing the volume gets no <code>/data/nfs</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Shared-state blockers (must be closed before cutover):</strong>
        <ul class="doc-body">
            <li><strong>Sessions:</strong> <code>Comserv/lib/Comserv.pm</code> loads <code>Store::Dummy</code> - a <em>test</em> store that keeps session data in per-process memory. With replicas&gt;=2 this logs users out on every round-robin hop, and does not survive a restart even today. The fix (switch to the already-configured <code>Store::DBIC</code>, pin the cookie name via <code>COMSERV_SESSION_COOKIE</code>) is staged but never switched on. This is evidence for the auth plan (AUTH-EVO), not implemented here; it needs a scheduled window.</li>
            <li><strong>/tmp cross-request state:</strong> <code>/tmp/whisper_job_*.status</code>, <code>/tmp/comserv_chat_progress_*</code>, <code>/tmp/comserv_security_scan.*</code> are written by one request and read by a later one - they break across pods. Job status/progress belong in the database (already keyed by id). Scratch used and discarded within one request may stay in pod-local <code>/tmp</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Done when:</strong> both pods <strong>Running</strong> and independently serving <code>/health</code>; no credential present in any image layer; anti-affinity confirmed (pods on different nodes).</p>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#why">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>

<h3 id="step4" class="doc-h3">Step 4 - Cutover / edge ingress</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Goal:</strong> route external traffic to the cluster ingress; retire the manual switch. <strong>Why:</strong> healthy pods that no user reaches protect no one.</p>
<ul class="doc-body">
    <li>Run the Deployment <em>alongside</em> the compose container; validate both serve identical responses.</li>
    <li>Switch the external entry to the cluster ingress. Use OPNsense ONLY as edge if its HAProxy is actually configured; otherwise terminate at the ingress directly.</li>
    <li>Rehearse rollback <em>before</em> cutover. Keep the compose path as an emergency escape hatch until Step 5 passes.</li>
</ul>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Done when:</strong> external traffic is served by the cluster with no user-visible change, and rollback is rehearsed.</p>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#why">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>

<h3 id="step5" class="doc-h3">Step 5 - Failover proof</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Goal:</strong> turn the "it survives a node loss" assumption into evidence. <strong>Why:</strong> untested HA has a poor record (the 2026-08-07 outage).</p>
<ul class="doc-body">
    <li>Graceful <code>kubectl drain</code> of .126 &rarr; site stays up on .198/.199; repeat draining .198 and .199.</li>
    <li><strong>Unplanned-failure test</strong> (pull power/network), not just a graceful drain.</li>
    <li>Record measured recovery time - first real input to the 99% budget.</li>
</ul>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Done when:</strong> an unplanned node kill leaves the site fully serving, recovery time recorded.</p>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#why">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>

<h3 id="step6" class="doc-h3">Step 6 - Database HA (.198 replica of .20)</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Goal:</strong> close the hard DB SPOF. <strong>Why:</strong> app HA without DB HA caps real availability at the availability of one database server; losing .20 takes down both app nodes at once, so 99% is arithmetically unreachable until this lands.</p>
<ul class="doc-body">
    <li>Assess .198 vs .20 for drift (hardware, MariaDB version parity, schema/row drift). .198's on-box MariaDB holds zero application schemas, so it is a clean replica base.</li>
    <li>Decide async replica (recommended first - near-zero risk to .20) vs Galera.</li>
    <li>Implement replication .20 &rarr; .198 with monitored lag; wire .198 as <strong>priority 2</strong> in the app's existing <code>db_config.json</code> failover (do not invent a new mechanism).</li>
    <li>Rehearse promotion.</li>
</ul>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Done when:</strong> .198 is a live replica of .20; priority-2 failover wired; replica-vs-backup decision recorded.</p>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#why">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>

<h3 id="step7" class="doc-h3">Step 7 - Automated propagation of a proven image</h3>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Goal:</strong> a build promoted on .199 reaches both production pods with <strong>no human action on any node</strong>, and a bad build is refused by the gate. <strong>Why:</strong> Steps 1-6 make rollout reliable; they do not make it automatic.</p>
<ul class="doc-body">
    <li>Promotion by <strong>immutable digest</strong> (never a floating <code>:latest</code>) through dev &rarr; staging &rarr; production.</li>
    <li>Rolling update with <code>maxUnavailable: 0</code>: a new pod must pass readiness before an old one is removed - a bad build stalls the rollout instead of taking the site down.</li>
    <li>Automatic rollback on failed rollout; <code>kubectl rollout undo</code> as the rehearsed manual path.</li>
    <li>Staging environment on the cluster so "proven" means proven somewhere real first.</li>
</ul>
<p class="doc-body"><strong>Done when:</strong> a promoted build reaches all pods with no human action on any node, and a deliberately bad build is refused before reaching users.</p>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#why">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>

<h2 id="vocab" class="doc-h2">4. Vocabulary - retired taxonomies</h2>
<p class="doc-body">v1.x used several dissimilar schemes for "steps" in one doc: Phases A-H, sub-projects #244-251 (which were <strong>never real</strong> - #202 has no children in the tracker), gate todos 1905-1910 + subtodos 2037-2044 + blockers 2046/2047, "rungs 0-4", and ad-hoc <code>DEPLOY_MODE</code> modes in deploy.sh. All are collapsed into the single <strong>Step 0-7</strong> sequence above. Consistent terms from here: <strong>node</strong> (not host/box/server), <strong>deploy</strong> (not push), <strong>app container</strong> (not web-prod/prod interchangeably), <strong>registry</strong> (one concept).</p>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#why">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>

<h2 id="spofs" class="doc-h2">5. Explicitly retained SPOFs (accepted, with rationale)</h2>
<ul class="doc-body">
    <li><strong>Registry host(s).</strong> If both .198 and .199 registries are down, running pods keep serving; only new pulls stop. Mitigation: push to both, keep node image caches, and the <code>docker save | ssh host docker load</code> escape hatch.</li>
    <li><strong>Kubernetes control plane on .50.</strong> Single server node today. Workloads keep running if the control plane blips; a full HA control plane (embedded-etcd, 3 nodes) is a follow-up, not a different distribution.</li>
    <li><strong>.199 is also the dev workstation.</strong> Tension: production capacity shares a machine rebooted for development. <strong>.198 resolves this</strong> - the app tier no longer depends on the workstation staying up.</li>
    <li><strong>OPNsense / WAN entry.</strong> Unchanged by this plan and not configured for HA today; if used, it is edge-only. The remaining accepted SPOF per the user's direction.</li>
    <li><strong>.20 primary DB</strong> - closed by Step 6.</li>
</ul>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#why">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>

<h2 id="maths" class="doc-h2">6. Availability maths</h2>
<table class="doc-table">
    <tr>
        <th class="doc-th">Stage</th>
        <th class="doc-th">Surviving faults</th>
        <th class="doc-th">Still fatal</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td class="doc-td">Today</td>
        <td class="doc-td">none - container, node, or DB loss = outage</td>
        <td class="doc-td">everything</td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="doc-tr-alt">
        <td class="doc-td">After Steps 0-5</td>
        <td class="doc-td">app crash, bad deploy, one app node down</td>
        <td class="doc-td">DB .20, both registries (deploys only), OPNsense/WAN</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td class="doc-td">After Step 6</td>
        <td class="doc-td">+ database host loss</td>
        <td class="doc-td">OPNsense / WAN entry, site-wide power</td>
    </tr>
</table>
<p class="doc-body">Steps 0-5 remove the <em>most frequent</em> causes of downtime (what bit us on 2026-08-07). <strong>Step 6 is required for a defensible 99% claim.</strong> The remaining single points after Step 6 (OPNsense/WAN, site power) should be acknowledged to clients rather than papered over.</p>
<p class="doc-backtop"><a href="#why">&#8593; Back to Top</a></p>

<h2 id="auth" class="doc-h2">7. Authentication - a DEPENDENCY, not owned here</h2>
<div class="doc-warning">
    <p class="doc-body">Scope correction: this plan states auth REQUIREMENTS and raises them on the auth plan; it does not redesign credentials. Owning project: <a href="/project/details?project_id=243" class="doc-link">#243 (AUTH-EVO)</a>, a sub-project of <a href="/project/details?project_id=201" class="doc-link">#201 SECURITY</a>. Owning doc: <a href="/Documentation/AuthenticationEvolutionPlan" class="doc-link">Authentication Evolution Plan</a> (marked ACTIVE - do not change without explicit permission).</p>
</div>
<table class="doc-table">
    <tr>
        <th class="doc-th">Req</th>
        <th class="doc-th">What HA needs</th>
        <th class="doc-th">Blocks</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td class="doc-td">AUTH-R1</td>
        <td class="doc-td">Credentials deliverable to a pod without a human placing a file on each node</td>
        <td class="doc-td">Step 3 / Step 7</td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="doc-tr-alt">
        <td class="doc-td">AUTH-R2</td>
        <td class="doc-td">Sessions valid across BOTH replicas (Store::Dummy fix + pinned cookie)</td>
        <td class="doc-td">Step 4</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td class="doc-td">AUTH-R3</td>
        <td class="doc-td">One credential set per connection, not per table</td>
        <td class="doc-td">Step 7</td>
    </tr>
    <tr class="doc-tr-alt">
        <td class="doc-td">AUTH-R4</td>
        <td class="doc-td">Managed credentials for .198 / new nodes</td>
        <td class="doc-td">Step 6</td>
    </tr>
</table>
